For minimal nonlinear CRNs of this type (such as Schlögl-type models), techniques exist — including WKB/eikonal large-deviation analysis, instanton calculations, spectral analysis of the master-equation generator, and Doi–Peliti field theory — that yield asymptotic expressions for the stationary distribution, the scaling of fluctuations near criticality, and the rates of noise-induced transitions between metastable phases.

Develop systematic mathematical methods that extend such fluctuation analyses to realistic, high-dimensional biochemical reaction networks (for example, signaling cascades, gene regulatory circuits, or metabolic modules performing a specified biological function), so as to compute or rigorously approximate, in the presence of nonequilibrium phase transitions:
(i) the stationary probability distribution $P_{\mathrm{ss}}(\mathbf{n})$ and its large-deviation rate function;
(ii) the scaling of chemical concentration fluctuations and correlation functions near the transition;
(iii) the mean first-passage times between metastable nonequilibrium phases; and
(iv) the associated entropy production and thermodynamic uncertainty relations along rare trajectories.

Provide a framework that is not restricted to one- or two-species toy models and that applies to nonlinear CRNs of arbitrary (finite) dimension carrying out a specified nontrivial biochemical task.
